zybo-qec-decoder
================

Standalone QEC decoder experiments for the Digilent Zybo Z7-10
(xc7z010clg400-1). No checkout of zybo-z7-tutorial is required.

Two independent designs are included:

* surface: distance-3 rotated surface-code Z memory, three rounds, 24 detectors;
  serial unweighted half-edge union-find and forest peeling in programmable logic.
* qec: distance-3 repetition memory, three rounds, eight detectors; a committed
  256-entry PyMatching-derived logical-flip lookup table in programmable logic.

The host uses Stim to simulate noisy quantum circuits. Only detector bits are
sent to the board, not the circuit or its true observable. ARM firmware bridges
the onboard USB UART to AXI GPIO and the PL decoder. The reply contains a logical
flip prediction and measured PL cycles. Compare with the matching Python decoder
for implementation correctness, PyMatching for an algorithm baseline, and Stim's
true observable for logical success. This is a completed-shot experiment, not a
quantum-processor interface, physical qubit correction or continuous feedback.

Quick software check (repository root)
-------------------------------------

  uv venv --python 3.12 qec/.venv
  uv pip install --python qec/.venv/bin/python -r qec/requirements.txt
  source qec/.venv/bin/activate
  make test
  make example
  python -m qec.surface.example --circuit
  python qec/host.py --mode loopback --shots 20000 --seed 90210

The example uses the committed noisy circuit and graph, selects the first
nonzero window from 256 seeded shots, and prints detector indices, packed
syndrome, true observable, union-find and PyMatching predictions, correction
edges and request bytes. The selection is illustrative, not an accuracy sample.
It makes no board connection unless --port is supplied. The loopback command is
software only. The circuit's four noise parameters are each 0.001: depolarization
after Clifford gates and before data rounds, measurement flips and reset flips.

Hardware/tool requirements
--------------------------

* Vivado and Vitis unified 2025.2 with Zynq-7000 support; Linux build tools and
  unzip. Source the installation's settings64.sh or put the tool bin directories
  on PATH. Vitis is needed only to build the ARM applications.
* Digilent Zybo Z7-10 board files, cable drivers and a powered board for loading.
  Set QEC_BOARD_REPO to the installed board_files directory if needed. Otherwise
  build_soc.tcl uses the adjacent AMD board directory when available, or Vivado's
  configured search paths. The board part is digilentinc.com:zybo-z7-10:part0:1.2.
* Onboard PROG/UART USB cable and serial-device permission for the USB-only SoC.
  An external adapter is NOT needed for this path. The separate PL-only UART
  requires 3.3 V TTL wiring: host TX -> JE1/V12, host RX <- JE2/W16, common ground.

Simulation (no board)
---------------------

  mkdir -p build
  make sim
  make sim_surface
  make sim_bridge
  QEC_RTL=1 python -m pytest qec/tests -q

make sim_surface writes the three logs required by the surface capture runner.
Keep its d3 Z run at 256 shots: the surface bridge bench uses that 423-case vector
file. Run sim_surface before sim_bridge. A PASS marker is required and any HDL
fatal invalidates a run even if xsim exits zero. All simulation runners accept
VIVADO_BIN for an alternate tool-bin directory and otherwise look on PATH before
the historical AMD 2025.2 home-directory fallback. Tool paths for make can also
be overridden with VIVADO, VITIS, XSCT, XVLOG, XELAB and XSIM.

Build a fresh surface image and firmware
---------------------------------------

  make build_soc > build/surface_build.log 2>&1
  make app > build/surface_firmware_build.log 2>&1

Defaults are SOC_TOP=top_surface_soc and APP=surface. Outputs:
  build/surface_soc.bit
  build/surface_soc.xsa
  build/vitis_ws/surface_app/build/surface_app.elf

Check build success, DRCs and routed timing. This makes a real PS+PL bitstream;
scripts/qec_build.tcl instead generates report-only core/PL-UART checkpoints.

Local verification is recorded in VERIFICATION.json. During initial validation,
the first fresh-workspace Vitis platform creation failed inside vendor repo.py
with NoneType.keys; an unchanged rerun and later builds succeeded. The cause is
not established. If this occurs, retain the log and retry make app; persistent
failures need investigation, not a fallback ELF from an old experiment.

Load and inspect one actual board request
----------------------------------------

The following replaces the running PL design and restarts ARM core 0 via JTAG.
It loads volatile state only, not flash or SD. Power loss requires reloading.

  python -m serial.tools.list_ports -v
  make run_soc > build/surface_program.log 2>&1
  make readback > build/surface_readback.log 2>&1
  python -m qec.surface.example --port /dev/ttyUSB1

Replace the port with the discovered board UART and close serial monitors first.
Require DONE=1, INIT_B=1 and CRC_ERROR=0 in the configuration-status log. That is
not a full bitstream-memory readback and does not itself verify decoding.

Surface request: C3 | sequence | length=03 | 3 syndrome bytes | XOR checksum
Surface reply:   3C | sequence | status | prediction | 4 cycle bytes | XOR checksum
All multi-byte fields are little-endian. Detector i maps to integer bit i.
For syndrome 2048 and sequence 0, the request is c3 00 03 00 08 00 c8.
A prediction of 1 means invert the logical-result interpretation, not apply a
physical qubit operation. At the configured 100 MHz FCLK, core ns = cycles * 10;
this excludes PS, AXI, USB, UART and host latency.

Full physical capture (fresh output directory)
----------------------------------------------

After all simulations, builds, program and readback above have succeeded:

  python qec/surface/host.py --port /dev/ttyUSB1 --board-id YOUR_BOARD_SERIAL \
    --shots 4096 --noise .001 --seed 20261107 --out build/surface-run

The board-id is an archive label, not device discovery. The runner validates
zero/single-detector/edge/dense windows, real Stim shots, malformed traffic and
PL reset recovery. It archives raw frames, circuit, source snapshot, manifests,
logs, reports and build artifacts. Do not skip prerequisites or change sources
between build and capture. Commit sources first: source snapshots use Git HEAD.
Missing prerequisite files can cause archiving to fail after transactions.
Only kind=stim rows support logical-error statistics. uf_mismatches must be zero;
disagreement with the different PyMatching algorithm is not itself an RTL bug.

Repetition alternative
----------------------

  make build_soc SOC_TOP=top_qec_soc > build/qec_soc_build.log 2>&1
  make app APP=qec > build/qec_firmware_build.log 2>&1
  make run_soc SOC_TOP=top_qec_soc APP=qec > build/qec_program.log 2>&1
  make readback > build/qec_readback.log 2>&1
  python qec/hardware_run.py --port /dev/ttyUSB1 --board-id YOUR_BOARD_SERIAL \
    --shots 4096 --noise .001 --seed 20261105 --out build/repetition-run

Build/load checks are the same. Surface and repetition bitstreams, firmware and
packet formats are incompatible; replace BOTH before switching. Repetition uses
four-byte requests and six-byte replies (see qec/transport.py). For synthesized
ROM verification: make reports MODE=core, then make netlist. MODE=uart reports the
external-PL-UART implementation; it does not generate a programmable SoC image.

Layout and provenance
---------------------

qec/                  Stim models, host transports, tests, LUT and results
qec/surface/          Surface graph/reference, example, RTL benches and results
rtl/common/           Decoder cores, PS bridges and required UART/synchronizers
rtl/top/              QEC SoC and standalone repetition UART top levels
rtl/tb/               Repetition/UART testbenches
scripts/              QEC simulation, synthesis, SoC build and JTAG loading
scripts/bd/           PS7/AXI GPIO block designs
constraints/          Zybo Z7-10 QEC and board pin/timing constraints
vitis/qec, surface/   Self-contained ARM firmware builds and sources

IMPORT.json records the original source revision, imported-file hashes and
explicit omissions. Archived result manifests remain unchanged; their source
revisions refer to zybo-z7-tutorial, NOT this repository. Historical Markdown and
source tarballs containing tutorial material were intentionally omitted and are
listed in IMPORT.json; those historical bundles are not complete local archives.
Retrieve omitted artifacts from the recorded original repository revision.
Archived measurements and binaries are historical evidence, not fresh validation
of this standalone revision. New captures archive this repository's own HEAD.

Basic FPGA/audio/tutorial projects and all Markdown files are intentionally
excluded. This plain-text README documents operational entry points only.
